Well, this is my third ascension, first one in about four or five years, 
and second class I've completed (The other two were both Valks). Had a 
Tourist achingly close a few weeks ago, only to have him fall apart on 
the astral plane, so I was quite pleased to find I could still actually 
do this ;)

Game was also played on nethack.alt.org, was just going to post a URL to 
the dumpfile, but I play so often on there I imagine it'll be gone 
before long, so the info follows at the bottom of this post for posterity.

The game started with a wand of wishing pretty early on, something I've 
not seen generated for so long I'd almost forgotten they could be 
randomly found. Given that the RNG was giving me a good head start for 
reasons unknown, I played this character pretty cautiously. AC -39 is 
possibly the lowest I've ever gone, and I achieved that with both the 
cash from Ludios and an awful lot of polypiling.

One other bit of randomly generated unusualness: My character tripped 
over the +0 Cleaver in one of the top levels of the gnomish mines, long 
before I'd started praying. Sure, I've seen it generated before, but 
never with a B, and it just struck me as kind of weird.

Only weapon I actually got from sacrificing was Vorpy, which I decided 
to use being as I had (luckily again) found a SoR in the first few 
levels, and was loathe to give it up for a two-handed weapon. I suppose 
I could have tried to get Frostbrand or something, but after my 
disastrous tourist I'd discovered how useful the PYEC was, so it took 
priority in my wishlist, despite it not liking me very much. :P Sunsword 
  I seem to remember I took from an Aleax, and shoved it in my bag for 
the rest of the game, just for the hell of it.

Easily the most prepared I've been for the endgame. Entered the planes 
already levitating, with a crystal ball and a scroll or two of gold 
detection, and even a couple of means of confusion. I really should 
start being prepared for things I know damned well are coming, it makes 
the last few moves a hell of a lot less hassle.

Astral was simply a case of barging my way to the front of the crowds, 
Famine went down to a couple of hits from my sword, and the first 
(middle) altar I tried was the right one.

So, there you have it. A lucky game in many ways, but I do seem to be 
getting more prepared for things when they do get unlucky.

~W


